skill: React

@ React Fundamentals | B
What is JSX?
= A syntax extension that lets you write HTML-like markup inside JavaScript
- A database query language
- A CSS preprocessor
- A browser plugin
? JSX is compiled to React.createElement calls (or the modern JSX runtime).

@ React Fundamentals | B
How does a component receive data from its parent?
= Through props
- Through global CSS
- Through the URL only
- Through the browser cookie jar only
? Props are read-only inputs passed to a component.

@ React Fundamentals | I
What is the virtual DOM?
= An in-memory representation of the UI that React compares to update the real DOM efficiently
- A second browser window
- A database for component state
- A CSS framework
? React diffs the new and old virtual trees and applies only the needed changes to the real DOM.

@ React Fundamentals | I
How do you conditionally render an element in JSX?
= Use JavaScript expressions such as a ternary or the && operator
- Use an if tag inside the markup
- Use the v-if attribute
- Use a CSS class called conditional
? JSX embeds JavaScript expressions in braces, so conditions use normal JavaScript operators.

@ Hooks | I
Which hook adds local state to a function component?
= useState
- useRef only
- useRouter
- useContent
? useState returns the current value and a function that updates it.

@ Hooks | I
When does a useEffect with an empty dependency array [] run?
= After the first render only
- After every render
- Before every render
- Never
? An empty dependency array means the effect has no reactive dependencies, so it runs once on mount.

@ Hooks | A
Which hook is intended for memoizing an expensive computed value?
= useMemo
- useState
- useId
- useLayout
? useMemo recalculates the value only when its dependencies change.

@ Hooks | A
What happens when a function is returned from a useEffect callback?
= It runs as cleanup before the effect re-runs or when the component unmounts
- It runs immediately during rendering
- It is ignored by React
- It replaces the component
? Cleanup functions are used to remove listeners, timers and subscriptions.

@ State and Props | I
What does calling setCount(count + 1) do?
= Schedules a re-render with the new state value
- Changes the variable immediately and synchronously everywhere
- Reloads the browser page
- Deletes the component
? State updates are scheduled, and React re-renders the component with the new value.

@ State and Props | I
Is it correct to mutate state directly, for example state.items.push(x)?
= No, create a new array or object and pass it to the setter
- Yes, React detects the change automatically
- Yes, but only inside useEffect
- No, because arrays cannot be stored in state
? React compares references, so updates should create new values rather than mutating existing ones.

@ State and Props | A
What is prop drilling, and one common way to avoid it?
= Passing props through many layers of components, avoided with Context
- Removing props from components, avoided with CSS
- Reading props from the database, avoided with SQL
- Mutating props, avoided with refs
? Context lets deeply nested components read shared values without passing props through every level.

@ Rendering and Lists | I
Why should list items rendered from an array have a key prop?
= It helps React identify which items changed, were added or removed
- It sets the CSS class of the item
- It encrypts the item
- It is required only for numbers
? Stable unique keys let React update lists efficiently and keep item state correct.

@ Rendering and Lists | A
What does React.StrictMode do during development?
= Highlights potential problems and helps detect unsafe side effects
- Makes the app faster in production
- Disables all hooks
- Prevents routing
? In development StrictMode intentionally re-runs some functions to expose impure code.

@ Routing and Forms | I
In a controlled input, what determines the displayed value?
= React state, supplied through value and updated in onChange
- The browser DOM only
- A cookie
- The CSS file
? A controlled component keeps the single source of truth in React state.

@ Routing and Forms | I
Which react-router-dom hook reads URL parameters such as :id in /users/:id?
= useParams
- useLocationParams
- useQueryId
- useRoute
? useParams returns an object with the dynamic segments of the matched route.

@ Data Fetching | I
Where do you typically call an API when a component first appears?
= Inside useEffect
- Directly in the component body on every render
- Inside the JSX markup
- In a CSS file
? Side effects such as network calls belong in effects or event handlers, not directly in rendering.
